      {
        "id": "E2",
        "kind": "body",
        "text": "Run GPU-Powered Black Hole Interferometry with NVIDIA cuPhoton",
        "sourceUrl": "https://developer.nvidia.com/blog/run-gpu-powered-black-hole-interferometry-with-nvidia-cuphoton/",
        "source": "NVIDIA Technical Blog",
        "publishedAt": "2026-11-13T21:17:17.000Z",
        "checkedAt": "2026-11-14T02:20:38.851Z"
      },
      {
        "id": "E3",
        "kind": "body",
        "text": "NVIDIA cuPhoton .xBeam accelerates radio interferometry correlation for the Event Horizon Telescope by processing voltage recordings into visibilities on GPUs.",
        "sourceUrl": "https://developer.nvidia.com/blog/run-gpu-powered-black-hole-interferometry-with-nvidia-cuphoton/",
        "source": "NVIDIA Technical Blog",
        "publishedAt": "2026-11-13T21:17:17.000Z",
        "checkedAt": "2026-11-14T02:20:38.851Z"
      },
      {
        "id": "E4",
        "kind": "body",
        "text": "On an NVIDIA Vera Rubin NVL72 system with 64 GPUs and GPUDirect Storage, xBeam correlated a 10 TB dataset in 70 seconds, achieving a 460x speedup over an x86 CPU baseline of 31,992 seconds.",
        "sourceUrl": "https://developer.nvidia.com/blog/run-gpu-powered-black-hole-interferometry-with-nvidia-cuphoton/",
        "source": "NVIDIA Technical Blog",
        "publishedAt": "2026-11-13T21:17:17.000Z",
        "checkedAt": "2026-11-14T02:20:38.851Z"
      },
      {
        "id": "E5",
        "kind": "body",
        "text": "Imaging black holes is challenging because they are distant and appear tiny in the sky—for example, M87*’s observed ring spans just 42 microarcseconds.",
        "sourceUrl": "https://developer.nvidia.com/blog/run-gpu-powered-black-hole-interferometry-with-nvidia-cuphoton/",
        "source": "NVIDIA Technical Blog",
        "publishedAt": "2026-11-13T21:17:17.000Z",
        "checkedAt": "2026-11-14T02:20:38.851Z"
      },
      {
        "id": "E6",
        "kind": "body",
        "text": "The Event Horizon Telescope (EHT) addresses this challenge by linking 13 radio observatory stations across locations, including Hawaii, Chile, Spain, Mexico, and the South Pole, through very-long-baseline interferometry (VLBI).",
        "sourceUrl": "https://developer.nvidia.com/blog/run-gpu-powered-black-hole-interferometry-with-nvidia-cuphoton/",
        "source": "NVIDIA Technical Blog",
        "publishedAt": "2026-11-13T21:17:17.000Z",
        "checkedAt": "2026-11-14T02:20:38.851Z"
      },
      {
        "id": "E7",
        "kind": "body",
        "text": "NVIDIA cuPhoton .xBeam performs GPU-based correlation for interferometry and organizes recorded data for parallel reads.",
        "sourceUrl": "https://developer.nvidia.com/blog/run-gpu-powered-black-hole-interferometry-with-nvidia-cuphoton/",
        "source": "NVIDIA Technical Blog",
        "publishedAt": "2026-11-13T21:17:17.000Z",
        "checkedAt": "2026-11-14T02:20:38.851Z"
      },
      {
        "id": "E8",
        "kind": "body",
        "text": "This method models the emitting gas as numerous compact, glowing blobs moving along orbits near a spinning black hole, optimized to match the observed visibilities. cuPhoton does not currently incorporate this second component.",
        "sourceUrl": "https://developer.nvidia.com/blog/run-gpu-powered-black-hole-interferometry-with-nvidia-cuphoton/",
        "source": "NVIDIA Technical Blog",
        "publishedAt": "2026-11-13T21:17:17.000Z",
        "checkedAt": "2026-11-14T02:20:38.851Z"
      },
      {
        "id": "E9",
        "kind": "body",
        "text": "Running on an NVIDIA Vera Rubin NVL72 system with 64 GPUs and GPUDirect Storage (GDS) in compatibility mode, xBeam processed a 10 TB dataset in 70 seconds —about 460x faster than an x86 CPU baseline of 31,992 seconds (8.89 hours).",
        "sourceUrl": "https://developer.nvidia.com/blog/run-gpu-powered-black-hole-interferometry-with-nvidia-cuphoton/",
        "source": "NVIDIA Technical Blog",
        "publishedAt": "2026-11-13T21:17:17.000Z",
        "checkedAt": "2026-11-14T02:20:38.851Z"
      },
      {
        "id": "E10",
        "kind": "body",
        "text": "The proposed Black Hole Explorer (BHEX) aims to measure this ring, and future ngEHT observations at 345 GHz could detect its signature through polarization measurements.",
        "sourceUrl": "https://developer.nvidia.com/blog/run-gpu-powered-black-hole-interferometry-with-nvidia-cuphoton/",
        "source": "NVIDIA Technical Blog",
        "publishedAt": "2026-11-13T21:17:17.000Z",
        "checkedAt": "2026-11-14T02:20:38.851Z"
      },
      {
        "id": "E11",
        "kind": "body",
        "text": "xBeam (part of cuPhoton v0.1.3) takes a normalized JSON job, schema version 1, or a supported DiFX .input file.",
        "sourceUrl": "https://developer.nvidia.com/blog/run-gpu-powered-black-hole-interferometry-with-nvidia-cuphoton/",
        "source": "NVIDIA Technical Blog",
        "publishedAt": "2026-11-13T21:17:17.000Z",
        "checkedAt": "2026-11-14T02:20:38.851Z"
      },
      {
        "id": "E12",
        "kind": "body",
        "text": "The run writes visibilities in the job’s output format, swin or xbeam-v1.",
        "sourceUrl": "https://developer.nvidia.com/blog/run-gpu-powered-black-hole-interferometry-with-nvidia-cuphoton/",
        "source": "NVIDIA Technical Blog",
        "publishedAt": "2026-11-13T21:17:17.000Z",
        "checkedAt": "2026-11-14T02:20:38.851Z"
      }
